1
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:03,100
Every morning,

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:05,540
before anyone at work opens a laptop,

3
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:06,580
thousands of data

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,660
pipelines have already run.

5
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:09,860
Something decided when

6
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:11,220
each one should start,

7
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,260
in what order,

8
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,420
and what to do when one fails.

9
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:15,820
In a lot of companies,

10
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:18,560
that something is Apache Airflow.

11
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:20,520
Hi, I'm Chris.

12
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:21,920
I've spent over 10 years

13
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:22,880
in data engineering

14
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:25,760
and interviewed more than 200 engineers.

15
00:00:25,780 --> 00:00:27,520
Now I run Stackle, where I teach

16
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:30,020
the skills that actually get you hired.

17
00:00:30,477 --> 00:00:32,217
Airflow is an open-source tool

18
00:00:32,237 --> 00:00:34,137
for orchestrating workflows.

19
00:00:34,157 --> 00:00:35,497
Orchestrating just means

20
00:00:35,517 --> 00:00:36,737
running the right task,

21
00:00:36,757 --> 00:00:39,097
at the right time, in the right order.

22
00:00:39,117 --> 00:00:41,737
It started at Airbnb in 2014,

23
00:00:41,757 --> 00:00:44,617
and today it's a top-level Apache project.

24
00:00:44,637 --> 00:00:45,817
You write each pipeline

25
00:00:45,837 --> 00:00:48,057
in Python, as a DAG.

26
00:00:48,077 --> 00:00:51,177
DAG just means directed acyclic graph.

27
00:00:51,197 --> 00:00:54,417
Each box is a task: extract the orders,

28
00:00:54,437 --> 00:00:55,177
transform them,

29
00:00:55,197 --> 00:00:57,017
and load them into the warehouse.

30
00:00:57,037 --> 00:00:58,857
The arrows are dependencies,

31
00:00:58,877 --> 00:00:59,897
so a task only

32
00:00:59,917 --> 00:01:02,777
runs after the tasks before it succeed.

33
00:01:02,797 --> 00:01:04,297
Now, the architecture.

34
00:01:04,317 --> 00:01:06,897
The DAG processor reads your Python files

35
00:01:06,917 --> 00:01:08,337
and saves each DAG

36
00:01:08,357 --> 00:01:10,057
to the metadata database.

37
00:01:10,077 --> 00:01:11,537
The scheduler checks that

38
00:01:11,557 --> 00:01:12,937
database, finds the tasks

39
00:01:12,957 --> 00:01:13,764
that are ready,

40
00:01:13,784 --> 00:01:15,577
and hands them to the executor.

41
00:01:15,597 --> 00:01:17,697
The executor sends them out to workers,

42
00:01:17,717 --> 00:01:19,817
and the workers do the actual work.

43
00:01:19,837 --> 00:01:21,697
Every result — queued,

44
00:01:21,717 --> 00:01:23,417
running, success or failed

45
00:01:23,437 --> 00:01:25,857
— goes back into the metadata database.

46
00:01:25,877 --> 00:01:27,657
And the web UI sits on top,

47
00:01:27,677 --> 00:01:29,377
so you can see every run,

48
00:01:29,397 --> 00:01:30,217
read the logs,

49
00:01:30,237 --> 00:01:32,990
and retry a failed task with one click.

50
00:01:33,010 --> 00:01:35,497
So when a team says, "we use Airflow,"

51
00:01:35,517 --> 00:01:37,817
it means their pipelines are code.

52
00:01:37,837 --> 00:01:38,977
They run on a schedule,

53
00:01:38,997 --> 00:01:40,057
in the right order,

54
00:01:40,077 --> 00:01:41,337
and when something breaks,

55
00:01:41,357 --> 00:01:43,177
they know exactly where.

56
00:01:43,197 --> 00:01:45,277
That's Apache Airflow.

57
00:01:47,337 --> 00:01:49,477
So, what should I explain next?

58
00:01:49,497 --> 00:01:50,637
Tell me in the comments below,

59
00:01:50,657 --> 00:01:52,857
and it could be the next episode.
